\noindent
Directional Hubble measurements from Migkas et~al.\cite{Migkas2020} fix an off-centre Sedov--Taylor\cite{Sedov1946,Taylor1950} / Lema\^itre--Tolman--Bondi (LTB)\cite{Lemaitre1933,Tolman1934,Bondi1947} shell geometry about a Centre of Expansion (CoE). The same geometry is then subjected to an independent consistency check against the cosmic microwave background (CMB)\cite{Planck2018VII}. This letter treats both the Migkas sky-dependent cluster Hubble rate and the Planck-level CMB isotropy of order \(10^{-5}\) as correct empirical facts. The CoE is the geometric origin of the radial shell structure; the observer (US) sits off-centre inside a thick ordinary-matter shell---the donut-universe configuration. We ask whether the gravitational multipole implied by that placement can be cancelled by a kill function: an electromagnetic temperature multipole (\(\Delta T/T\) of kinematic--Doppler character) opposite in sign to the gravitational last-scattering multipole and sharing its angular shape. Apparent late-time accelerated expansion is, in this geometry, an illusion requiring neither dark energy nor outward shell acceleration.

\section{Problem and stance}
Migkas et~al.\ report a sky-dependent cluster Hubble rate. Standard cosmology assumes large-scale isotropy, and the CMB is isotropic to \(\sim10^{-5}\)\cite{Planck2018VII}. This letter takes both seriously: the cosmological principle alone does not account for the Migkas anisotropy, and the goal is coexistence with a flat last-scattering sky, not replacement of either dataset. The LTB origin is the CoE, not US; the observer sits at \(r=D\), and anisotropy is an off-centre sampling of \(\beta(r)\).

\section{Best-fit geometry}
Local Hubble velocity is free-fall toward the CoE minus our motion,
\(\vec{V}_H=\vec{V}_g-\vec{V}_O\).
The observer (``US'') sits inside a thick shell; vision radius \(R\approx14.3\,\mathrm{Gpc}\); shell rule \(D\gtrsim14.7\,\mathrm{Gpc}\). Preferred relativistic fit: \(D\approx32.9\,\mathrm{Gpc}\), \(R_{\mathrm{in}}\approx18.1\,\mathrm{Gpc}\), \(R_{\mathrm{out}}\approx49.7\,\mathrm{Gpc}\).

