\section{Time evolution and the CMB}
In the donut-universe picture the CoE is fixed while thick shells expand outward faster than light in a coordinate sense; the sphere of events visible to us grows at \(c\). Recombination is a spherical surface of fixed physical radius around the CoE. The observer remains mid-shell between the inner and outer edges (S1 and S2). The Hubble-bubble volume about US satisfies \(V_{\mathrm{HB}}=0\) until the observer passes \(D_{\mathrm{recomb}}\); afterward the visible sphere grows at \(c\). Scale may be tracked conceptually through \(V_{\mathrm{HB}}(t)\), \(V_{\mathrm{S1}}(t)\), \(V_{\mathrm{S2}}(t)\) and their fractions relative to the shell annulus. Geometry alone is on the left below; stage prose for the letter body is on the right.

