\section{What is interesting}
The Migkas anisotropy is a gravitational effect: free-fall relative to an off-centre CoE. On the last-scattering sky that same gravity produces a temperature multipole. Separately, a pure electromagnetic Doppler pattern---from radial motion of emitting surfaces---has nearly the same angular shape. The \emph{kill function} is that electromagnetic temperature multipole written opposite in sign to the gravitational residual. Today's result is that this gravitational kill tracks the electromagnetic Doppler morphology across the distances scanned. That morphological agreement is unexpected if gravity and the photon bath are unrelated, and it suggests a geometric route to CMB flatness that does not discard the Migkas fit. The open light-cone problem is whether the match can be driven to the \(10^{-5}\) amplitude required by Planck.

Apparent late-time accelerated expansion is an illusion in this geometry: no dark energy and no actual outward acceleration of the shell are required. Off-centre sampling of the expansion field produces the appearance. A contracting universe under analogous sampling would also show, on average, the appearance of accelerated expansion, but with a different angular signature---a natural observational distinguisher.

\section{What we do next}
\begin{enumerate}\setlength{\itemsep}{2pt}\setlength{\parsep}{0pt}
\item Evolve \(D(t)\), \(R_{\mathrm{in}}(t)\), and \(R_{\mathrm{out}}(t)\) from the present back through recombination, with the observer mid-shell.
\item Integrate null geodesics on that history to obtain \(T(\theta)\) on our sky, including gravitational and kinematic contributions together.
\item Ask whether a shared bulk velocity of the emitting surfaces, plus the gravitational multipole, can keep \(\ell\ge2\) below \(\sim10^{-5}\) while the low-redshift \(A(\ell)\) pattern remains Migkas-compatible.
\item If a residual remains, identify the smallest additional degree of freedom (timing of recombination, shell thickness history, or a mild partner multipole) that removes it without reintroducing a fixed-curve conflict across redshift slices.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Bottom line}
Migkas fixes an off-centre CoE+shell---the donut-universe geometry with US at \(r=D\)---that fits directional Hubble across slices. Gravity on that geometry predicts a last-scattering multipole whose kill function---an electromagnetic temperature multipole opposite in sign and matching in angular shape---tracks the kinematic Doppler pattern from the same radii. Apparent late-time acceleration is an illusion of off-centre sampling; a contracting geometry would share the average appearance but differ in angular signature. The open problem is no longer whether the gravitational and electromagnetic shapes talk to each other---they do---but whether a full time-dependent light-cone calculation can make them cancel at the \(10^{-5}\) level without spoiling the Migkas anisotropy. The goal is coexistence of both datasets under one off-centre shell geometry.



